{"type":"text","timestamp":1788993893309,"sessionID":"ses_f77ade3feffeYe56P6hqdU6Mjz","part":{"id":"prt_0885858e3001GnKE1F1O96K4AK","messageID":"msg_0885854a8001PQzlHqfEjkyuJN","sessionID":"ses_f77ade3feffeYe56P6hqdU6Mjz","type":"text","text":"The deliverable is ready: `./switchcheck <path>` runs directly via a bash shebang that dispatches to `switchcheck.py`. All 13 files in `cases/` were tested and each produces exactly the diagnostic set implied by its scenario (empty for well-formed programs, the single relevant tag otherwise):\n\n- `switch_inexhaustive` for the missing-clause and missing-tuple-combo cases\n- `unreachable_arm` for the shadowed-arm case\n- `rebinding` for the guarded-arm reusing the parameter name\n- `return_not_declared`, `arg_not_accepted`, `switch_in_guard`, `unbound_variable`, `vacuous_arm`, `unsatisfiable_arm_guard` each firing exactly once for their respective cases\n- No output for the two well-formed cases (closed exhaustive atoms, open catch-all, and the exhaustive bool-tuple case)\n\nImplementation notes (for transparency, not printed by the tool):\n- Pure Python 3 (no dependencies), parses type declarations (atom unions), function signatures, and clause/switch bodies via depth-aware bracket parsing rather than a full grammar.\n- Exhaustiveness is computed by enumeration over closed domains (atom unions, `bool`, and tuples of these) and by interval subtraction for `int`; open types (`atom`, `term`, unenumerated `int`) are treated conservatively — a catch-all is accepted and no diagnostic is guessed when the checker can'"'"'t prove non-exhaustiveness.\n- Unreadable guards (arbitrary calls, variable-to-variable comparisons) are treated as crediting nothing toward coverage but are never used to declare an arm dead, matching the spec'"'"'s \"credits nothing, but never blamed for the checker'"'"'s own ignorance\" rule.","time":{"start":1788993886435,"end":1788993893248}}}
